[Music]  imagine imagine that you're seeing  things that aren't there  and you're confused about time and place  and you quickly forget things that  happen to you you're hallucinating  you're delirious  you're suffering from amnesia if you  were to walk into a doctor's office with  any of these symptoms you'd be declared  psychopathic maybe even placed in an  institution and yet all of us were in  this state 12 hours ago we were sleeping  and that's the seeming paradox of sleep  this state that was selected by Nature  over the course of 3.6 million years and  it makes no sense think about from an  evolutionary perspective when you're  sleeping you're not finding food  you're clearly now reproduce not even  you're not even fending for yourself  you're totally vulnerable on any of  these grounds sleep should have been  strongly selected against yet here we  are three and a half million years later  spending a third of our lives in this  state of flagrant psychosis if sleep  doesn't serve some truly vital function  then it's the biggest mistake the  evolutionary process ever made we now  know that sleep does have many vital  functions from cementing learning and  consolidating memory to regulating  emotion and fostering the association of  disparate experiences and we also know  that the vast majority of us need  between seven to nine hours of sleep per  night to function properly so why do we  have such a hard time getting the amount  of sleep our brain so crave because we  live in a culture that prices sleep  machismo and we perpetuate a series of  myths so let's talk about a couple of  them so first myth you might say I can  go with five or six hours of sleep ER  and I didn't feel just fine and you'd be  partially right there are some people  that have a rare genetic mutation that  allows them to get by with five hours or  less of sleep each night with  showing any signs of impairment however  according to dr. Thomas Ruth from the  Henry Ford Hospital in Detroit the  proportion of the population for which  this is the case rounded to the nearest  whole number is zero it's negligible for  the rest of us insufficient sleep can  have serious consequences let me tell  you about an experiment dr. David  dentist the University of Pennsylvania  took two groups of people one of whom  was sleep deprived the other of whom was  drunk drunk too the legal driving limit  so pointer weight percent of Plaut  alcohol and he gave these both of these  groups a concentration test to assess  cognitive performance and what he found  was that after just 19 hours of being  awake 19 hours the sleep deprived group  was as cognitively impaired as the group  that was legally drunk so think about  what that means imagine you wake up on a  Friday at 7 a.m. and you go to work and  you spend your day at work then maybe  you go out on the evening on a date or  with friends typical Friday and even if  you drink no alcohol whatsoever by the  time you're driving back home at 2:00 in  the morning you're essentially a drunk  driver but you might say wait you know I  really do get five or six hours of sleep  per night in fact I just got six hours  of sleep over the past few nights and I  feel great I feel fantastic am i  delusional and the answer is yes  yes you are dr. dangers experiment had a  twist  and when he asked the sleep-deprived  group how cognitively impaired they felt  they significantly underestimated their  degree of disability in fact their  self-assessment was a terrible miserable  predictor of just how bad they performed  another major problem tied to this is  something called baseline resetting and  it's been shown that after months or  years of chronic sleep deprivation  people actually acclimate to their  impaired performance and reduced energy  levels never even realizing that they're  failing to maximize the potential of  mind and body  epidemiological studies suggest that  millions of people spend years of their  lives living like this so bottom line is  we don't know how sleep-deprived we are  when we're sleep-deprived and that's the  scariest part of it all now 1/2 a second  myth we've all probably said at some  point in our lives you know I'll just  sleep it off over the weekend and the  research shows that just isn't the case  or brains are incapable of that so back  to doctor didn't just study after the  initial test he took those  sleep-deprived group and gave them three  full recovery nights so three full  nights of sleep then tested them again  what do you think happened the second  time around turns out that even after  3/4 recovery night's performance still  did not return to the original baseline  levels sleep just doesn't work like that  fact we now know that lack of sleep  builds up and compounds over time and  research even links it to some of the  most feared diseases in the developed  world one of them is Alzheimer's  Alzheimer's is triggered by a metabolic  toxin called beta-amyloid which builds  up naturally in our brains over the  course of the day and sleep offers a  cleansing mechanism and so the last  sleep you're having across your lifespan  though more of that toxic protein is  building up night after night year after  year so when sufficient sleep is now  considered one of the most significant  lifestyle factors determining whether or  not you'll develop the disease  and for that reason doctor Walker from  Berkeley goes as far as to call  wakefulness a state of low-level brain  damage whereas sleep offers a repertory  function okay so sleep matters I hope  you'll agree with me on that so what's  the upshot what can we do to help combat  sleep machismo and address the sleepless  epidemic so first off in an individual  level there's a ton of research and  literature out there on on insomnia and  how to improve your sleep so I won't  delve too much into it all I'll say is  that by far the best thing you can do  for your sleep is simply give yourself  an eight-hour window no it takes a  meaningful lifestyle change but it's  totally worth it on the business level  despite what we now know about sleep  sleep machismo remains pervasive in fact  lack of sleep is often even boasted as a  badge of honor as an example in my  former job as a consultant one of the  lead partners in the firm loved to have  sleep as one of his work life balance  kpi's and his aspirational metric to  gates almost how much sleep he was  getting was having no less than four and  a half hours of sleep per night on  average four and a half hours and that  was the aspiration  of course that mindset trickle down to  the rest of the team and the broader  firmament reinforce the stigmatization  of sleep so from those overnight red-eye  flights to the always on expectation and  the indefinite work schedules that often  drag beyond midnight I believe that  sleep merits a radical rethink of  policies and priorities in the business  world because awareness drives action  and change has to come from the top  we've all heard the old maxim you can  sleep when you're dead  let me tell you that is mortally unwise  advice we now know that the shorter you  sleep the shorter you live and the  quality of that now shorter life will be  much worse sleep is not a paradox it is  not a luxury research for those of us  without busy lives it is a biological  necessity and it is critical I sincerely  hope that you'll reflect on some of  these learnings and if you don't please  just sleep on it  [Applause]  [Music]

